    "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Graph_theory",
    "summary": "This article is about Mathematical field on vertices connected by edges. For graphs of mathematical functions, see Graph of a function . For other uses, see Graph (disambiguation) . Area of discrete mathematics A graph with 6 vertices and 7 edges In mathematics and computer science , graph theory is the study of graphs , which are mathematical structures used to model pairwise relations between objects. A graph in this context is made up of vertices (also called nodes or points) which are connected by edges (also called arcs, links, or lines). A distinction is made between undirected graphs, where edges link two vertices symmetrically, and directed graphs , where edges link two vertices asymmetrically. Graphs are one of the principal objects of study in discrete mathematics .",
    "text": "This article is about Mathematical field on vertices connected by edges. For graphs of mathematical functions, see Graph of a function . For other uses, see Graph (disambiguation) . Area of discrete mathematics A graph with 6 vertices and 7 edges In mathematics and computer science , graph theory is the study of graphs , which are mathematical structures used to model pairwise relations between objects. A graph in this context is made up of vertices (also called nodes or points) which are connected by edges (also called arcs, links, or lines). A distinction is made between undirected graphs, where edges link two vertices symmetrically, and directed graphs , where edges link two vertices asymmetrically. Graphs are one of the principal objects of study in discrete mathematics .",
