        "title": "Graph theory",
        "pageid": 12401,
        "revid": 1374306193,
        "text": "<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r1368532237\">.mw-parser-output .hatnote{font-style:italic}.mw-parser-output div.hatnote{padding-left:1.6em;margin-bottom:0.5em}.mw-parser-output .hatnote i{font-style:normal}.mw-parser-output .hatnote+.mw-empty-elt+.hatnote{margin-top:-0.5em}@media print{body.ns-0 .mw-parser-output .hatnote{display:none!important}}</style><div role=\"note\" class=\"hatnote navigation-not-searchable\">This article is about Mathematical field on vertices connected by edges. For graphs of mathematical functions, see <a href=\"/wiki/Graph_of_a_function\" title=\"Graph of a function\">Graph of a function</a>. For other uses, see <a href=\"/wiki/Graph_(disambiguation)\" class=\"mw-redirect mw-disambig\" title=\"Graph (disambiguation)\">Graph (disambiguation)</a>.</div>\n<div class=\"shortdescription nomobile noexcerpt noprint searchaux\" style=\"display:none\">Area of discrete mathematics</div>\n<figure class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File/Thumb\"><a href=\"/wiki/File:Example_of_simple_undirected_graph_3.svg\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Example_of_simple_undirected_graph_3.svg/250px-Example_of_simple_undirected_graph_3.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"200\" height=\"132\" style=\"--mw-file-upright: 0.8\" class=\"mw-file-element mw-file-upright\" srcset=\"//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Example_of_simple_undirected_graph_3.svg/500px-Example_of_simple_undirected_graph_3.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x\" data-file-width=\"97\" data-file-height=\"64\" /></a><figcaption>A graph with 6 vertices and 7 edges</figcaption></figure>\n<p>In <a href=\"/wiki/Mathematics\" title=\"Mathematics\">mathematics</a> and <a href=\"/wiki/Computer_science\" title=\"Computer science\">computer science</a>, <b>graph theory</b> is the study of <a href=\"/wiki/Graph_(discrete_mathematics)\" title=\"Graph (discrete mathematics)\">graphs</a>, which are <a href=\"/wiki/Mathematical_structures\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Mathematical structures\">mathematical structures</a> used to model pairwise relations between objects. A graph in this context is made up of <a href=\"/wiki/Vertex_(graph_theory)\" title=\"Vertex (graph theory)\">vertices</a> (also called nodes or points) which are connected by <a href=\"/wiki/Glossary_of_graph_theory_terms#edge\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Glossary of graph theory terms\">edges</a> (also called arcs, links, or lines). A distinction is made between undirected graphs, where edges link two vertices symmetrically, and <a href=\"/wiki/Directed_graph\" title=\"Directed graph\">directed graphs</a>, where edges link two vertices asymmetrically. Graphs are one of the principal objects of study in <a href=\"/wiki/Discrete_mathematics\" title=\"Discrete mathematics\">discrete mathematics</a>.\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nParsed by mw‐api‐ext.eqiad.main‐77d9d4c448‐626tg\nCached time: 20260918164201\nCache expiry: 2592000\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [vary‐revision‐sha1]\nCPU time usage: 0.092 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.146 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 150/1000000\nRevision size: 963/2097152 bytes\nPost‐expand include size: 1559/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 368/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 8/100\nExpensive parser function count: 2/500\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 409/5000000 bytes\nLua time usage: 0.059/10.000 seconds\nLua memory usage: 1638122/52428800 bytes\nNumber of Wikibase entities loaded: 0/500\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%  125.852      1 -total\n 61.10%   76.899      1 Template:About\n 38.78%   48.807      1 Template:Short_description\n 16.54%   